          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I keep getting urine infections.",
          "answer": "Urinary tract infections (UTIs) are bacterial infections of the urinary system. Most affect the bladder and urethra, which is called a lower tract infection or cystitis. *Escherichia coli* causes most of them (Urinary tract infection, General clinical reference). Infection that reaches the kidney is a more serious illness, called pyelonephritis (same source).\n\nGuidance treats infections differently depending on whether they are \"uncomplicated\" (in a non-pregnant adult with a normal urinary tract and normal immune function) or \"complicated\" (for example, in pregnancy, in men, with a urinary abnormality, a catheter, kidney stones, poorly controlled diabetes, or a weakened immune system) (same source). Guidance also says a urine culture is useful when an infection recurs (Uncomplicated urinary tract infection guideline). Diabetes is listed as a possible cause of recurrent infections (Type 2 diabetes reference). That is one reason a clinician may want to check for underlying causes.\n\nThe sources I searched did not include specific prevention or lifestyle advice for recurrent UTIs, so I can't give you a sourced list of measures. Please ask a clinician or pharmacist about prevention.\n\n**Please see a doctor** to look for underlying causes and to discuss treatment options. Tell them how often you get infections and how each one has been treated.\n\n**Seek urgent care now** if you have a fever, shaking chills, back or side pain, vomiting, confusion, or you feel unusually unwell. These can be signs of a kidney infection or of sepsis, a life-threatening response to infection (Sepsis guidance).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
