          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do I need an antibiotic every time?",
          "answer": "Not every time. Antibiotics only work against bacteria. Many common infections, including colds, most sore throats, and flu, are caused by viruses, and antibiotics do not shorten them. The sources describe antibiotics as carrying real harms: side effects, allergic reactions, disruption of the normal gut bacteria, *C. difficile* infection, and antibiotic resistance (Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship guidance; Upper respiratory infection, General clinical reference). Guidance treats not prescribing them for viral illness as an active recommendation (Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship guidance).\n\nFor urinary infections, the sources say a clinician usually decides on treatment based on the likely cause and local resistance patterns. A urine culture (a lab test to identify the bacteria) is recommended when it would change treatment. That includes recurrent infections, pregnancy, suspected kidney involvement, symptoms that persist or return within a few weeks of treatment, or recent antibiotic use or travel (Uncomplicated urinary tract infection guideline). Some guidance also supports delayed or no antibiotics for mild cystitis as a shared decision with a clinician, though other guidance keeps immediate antibiotics as the default (same guideline). A clinician will weigh these options with you.\n\nThe sources do not say when an antibiotic is right for your own situation. Please ask a clinician, and tell them how often you have infections and how each was treated.\n\n**Seek urgent care now** if you have fever with back or side pain, shaking chills, vomiting that stops you drinking, confusion, or you feel very unwell. These can signal a kidney infection or sepsis (Urinary tract infection, General clinical reference; Sepsis guidance).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
